# Repository-wide line-ending policy.
#
# ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING: in .gitattributes THE LAST MATCHING LINE WINS. The
# golden exemption below must therefore stay AFTER the `*` rule, never before
# it. Verified empirically, with core.autocrlf=true:
#     exemption placed BEFORE `* text=auto` -> `git check-attr text` = "auto"
#                                              (exemption OVERRIDDEN, file
#                                               normalized, transport pin broken)
#     exemption placed AFTER  `* text=auto` -> `git check-attr text` = "unset"
#                                              (exemption holds)
# Do not reorder these lines. The ordering is machine-enforced by
# test_golden_exemption_survives_repo_wide_normalization in
# tests/test_agent_studio_capability_discovery_golden.py, so a reordering fails
# the suite rather than silently reverting a pin. That test is the ONLY detector:
# because `eol=lf` below forces LF in the working tree, a defeated exemption
# produces no CRLF and no pin movement, so the byte-level checks cannot see it.
#
# `eol=lf` is deliberate and not cosmetic. `text=auto` alone normalizes what is
# STORED; `eol=lf` also forces LF in the WORKING TREE. That is what actually
# prevents the original defect class here -- development on Windows with
# core.autocrlf=true, validated on Linux CI -- and it is required for the
# shell scripts under scripts/, which break outright with CRLF.
#
# Landing this is byte-safe: a sweep of the union of all non-dependabot branch
# tips (793 unique blobs) found ZERO stored-CRLF text files, so this rule is a
# no-op on stored bytes at every tip and cannot break an existing digest pin.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The only genuine binary in the tree. Marked explicitly rather than relying on
# git's content-detection heuristic, for the same reason the golden below
# carries an explicit rule: "probably detected correctly" is not a control.
*.ico binary

# DO NOT REMOVE THIS EXEMPTION, AND DO NOT MOVE IT ABOVE THE `*` RULE.
#
# tests/golden/provider-adapter-openapi.v7.json is a pinned snapshot of the
# integration provider's public flat-v7 OpenAPI contract (provider commit
# b2745459). Its test asserts two pins:
#   * transport pin -- SHA-256 of the exact bytes, which must equal the digest
#     of the provider's own blob (7a484f39...) so the baseline is independently
#     verifiable against provider source rather than against whoever's checkout
#     recorded it;
#   * semantic pin -- a content-canonical (parsed, sorted-key) digest.
#
# The provider stores that contract with LF endings. Because this repository is
# developed on Windows where core.autocrlf=true and validated in CI on Linux,
# WITHOUT this `-text` rule git would rewrite the file on checkout. That
# silently changes its bytes, moves the transport pin from 7a484f39... to
# 4b3830bc..., and makes the golden test report drift against the provider that
# does not actually exist -- while appearing to pass against a baseline that is
# merely an artifact of the recording checkout.
#
# `-text` means: git must never perform end-of-line conversion on this file in
# either direction. It is a deliberate correctness control, not an oversight or
# a stray inconsistency to be tidied away.
tests/golden/provider-adapter-openapi.v7.json -text
